<figure class="flex flex-col gap-4 border-2 border-ink p-4 sm:p-6">
<p class="font-bold">An extra metre of string raises the loop by about 16 centimetres everywhere.</p>
<svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" class="mx-auto w-full max-w-lg" role="img" aria-labelledby="girdle-title girdle-desc">
<title id="girdle-title">A circular Earth with its string raised by a uniform gap</title><desc id="girdle-desc">An orange outer circle surrounds the Earth. The diagram exaggerates the tiny 16 centimetre gap. The increase in circumference is one metre.</desc>
<circle cx="212" cy="122" r="91" fill="#387a91" fill-opacity=".25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"/>
<circle cx="212" cy="122" r="112" fill="none" stroke="#bd4b37" stroke-width="3"/>
<g fill="currentColor" font-size="17"><text x="346" y="71">Extra metre</text><text x="346" y="92">of string</text><text x="346" y="160">Gap at every</text><text x="346" y="181">point: 0.159 m</text></g>
<path d="M304 100 L340 77 M303 145 L340 154" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5"/>
</svg>
<div class="text-base font-bold">Extra radius = 1 m ÷ (2π) ≈ 0.159 m</div>
<figcaption class="text-base leading-normal">Circumference is 2π times radius. Add 1 m to any circle’s circumference and its radius grows by 1/(2π) m, no matter how large the circle was. The gap here is hugely exaggerated so it can be seen.</figcaption></figure>